## Addendum 2026-10-30: Technical Analysis Mastery

Binding distilled rules from the 2026-10-30 technical analysis mastery study. These rules bind every read run under this skill.

1. Run the reading order in sequence, every time: frame and series registry, one sentence gestalt, weekly regime, cycle position, daily structure, location, volatility state, volume verdict, deciding level, then indicators, then the plan.
2. Name the cycle position before quoting any indicator.
3. Transcribe key values before interpreting anything, and reconcile any disagreement between the image and the computed geometry before narrating.
4. Use confirmed 5 bar fractal pivots only, and keep each pivot's extreme time separate from its availability time.
5. Anchor Fibonacci work wick to wick on a named swing, stated with its dates and prices.
6. Judge every trigger and every invalidation on closes, never on wicks: a wick through a zone does not kill it, a close below the bottom of the zone does.
7. Keep the Echo wave invariants absolute: wave 2 never retraces all of wave 1, wave 3 is never the shortest of waves 1, 3 and 5, and wave 4 does not overlap wave 1 in a standard impulse; a count that breaks a rule is invalidated, never relabelled.
8. Use the retracement tool for pullback depth on waves 2 and 4 and post cycle corrections, and the three point trend based extension tool for targets on waves 3, 5 and C; never substitute one tool's 1.618 for the other's.
9. Treat the Echo depth bands as the retracement map: 0.5 to 0.618 shallow, 0.618 to 0.786 the wave 2 sweet spot, 0.786 to 0.887 deep; downgrade conviction beyond 0.886 instead of extending the zone.
10. Score the Echo target ladder by role: T1 at the 1.272 extension stall watch, the objective at the 1.618 extension, the extended case at 2.0 to 2.618 only when momentum carries through 1.618.
11. Define confluence as a count: independent constructions landing inside the same ATR scaled band, scored from Fibonacci retracement, Fibonacci extension, moving average cluster, trendline arrival, prior pivot shelf and round number.
12. Call a lone Fibonacci level a watch level, never a zone; promote a band to a buy zone only with at least two independent constructions inside it.
13. Treat arrival at a zone as a watch state, and require a reaction on closes, with bounce volume above the pullback mean, before calling a setup.
14. Draw at most two trendlines, anchored on confirmed major pivots spanning the whole pattern; report a line price has closed through as dead and never redraw it to fit.
15. Count level touches and cluster evidence by independent episode, never by repeated bars inside one episode.
16. Count RSI or MACD divergence only at pattern extremes, let it arm a level only, and name the average reclaim or loss or the zone reaction as the trigger in advance with its price.
17. Enforce the stop distance floor: keep invalidation at least 1.5 ATR from the entry reference, with 1.5 to 2.5 ATR as the working band for swing reads, and state the ATR at read time.
18. Label every target by role and by method: recorded level, pattern measured move, or projected; never print a target with neither label.
19. Compute and print R to T1 and R to the objective, judge the setup on R to the objective, and exclude any setup with R below 1 to its objective from boarding.
20. Draw every plan chart to the annotation standard: shaded labelled buy zone, labelled invalidation line with price, targets labelled by role, the named pattern drawn on the price pane, at most two labelled trendlines, only the averages the call cites, dashed primary and alternate expected paths into reserved future space, volume, RSI and MACD panes with divergences drawn only where the record carries them, the expiry clock, and R on the chart.
21. Cure recency by writing the weekly regime before describing the daily, and by transcribing full pattern anchors before the last bar.
22. Cure anchoring by computing geometry blind, scoring every candidate zone including the first one named, and drawing the alternate scenario with its own terminus on every chart.
23. Cure forced patterns with confirmation law: no pattern is a call before its confirmation close, run the family checklist out loud, run the trap screen on every claimed continuation, and fall back to structure language when a rule fails.
24. Cure wick versus close confusion by writing the wick and close rule for each level into its trigger and invalidation sentences before price reaches the level.
25. Cure invented base rates by quoting only the published genome calibration table or the reader's own scored log, or by stating that no base rate is established.
26. Answer the two questions separately in every read: whether the name is still going up, and whether it is safe to buy here; never let a yes to the first imply a yes to the second.

